\details{
The copula engine is SYDNI's dense dependence-preservation engine. It models
joint association among synthesized variables through subgroup-specific
Gaussian copulas, while reconstructing each output variable through empirical
margins rather than assuming normally distributed synthetic values.

Let \eqn{D = \{x_i\}_{i=1}^{n_{\mathrm{obs}}}} be the observed data,
\eqn{V = \{v_1,\ldots,v_{p_V}\}} be the synthesized variables, and
\eqn{C = \{c_1,\ldots,c_q\}} be optional covariates. The requested synthetic
sample size is \eqn{n_{\mathrm{aug}}}. If \code{covariates} are supplied,
each unique covariate combination defines a subgroup:
\deqn{
  g_i = \mathrm{interaction}(c_{i1},\ldots,c_{iq}).
}
If no covariates are supplied, all rows belong to a single group.

The engine proceeds in seven steps:

\enumerate{
  \item Estimate a global shrinkage correlation matrix from the observed
    synthesized variables:
    \deqn{
      R_{\mathrm{global}} =
      \mathrm{cor.shrink}(D[V]).
    }
    This matrix is used as a stable dependence target for small subgroups.
    If \code{measurement_blocks} are supplied, this observed global matrix is
    also retained as the measurement reference. Between-block correlations
    are attenuated by \code{between_block_shrink}, but each within-block
    submatrix is restored from the observed global reference before copula
    simulation. In effect, the engine applies conservative shrinkage to
    structural relations among measurement families while protecting the
    internal item relations that support reliability, omega, and AVE.

  \item For each subgroup \eqn{g}, compute its observed size
    \eqn{n_g}, observed proportion \eqn{\pi_g = n_g / n_{\mathrm{obs}}},
    preliminary noisy allocation
    \eqn{a_g = \lceil \pi_g n_{\mathrm{aug}} \rceil}, and noisy feeder
    target size
    \deqn{
      n_g^{\mathrm{target}} =
      \max\left(
        a_g,\;
        m_{\mathrm{feed}} p_V
      \right).
    }
    Here \eqn{m_{\mathrm{feed}}} is
    \code{engine_control$feeder_min_multiplier}, with default 10. The number
    of noisy feeder rows is
    \deqn{
      n_g^{\mathrm{syn}} =
      \begin{cases}
      a_g, & n_g \ge n_g^{\mathrm{target}},\\
      n_g^{\mathrm{target}} - n_g, & n_g < n_g^{\mathrm{target}}.
      \end{cases}
    }
    This restores the original copula-engine philosophy that even
    well-populated cells receive noisy feeder rows, rather than using purely
    observed rows whenever the observed subgroup is already large.

  \item Bootstrap \eqn{n_g^{\mathrm{syn}}} additional rows from each subgroup
    with replacement and inject noise into the synthesized columns only.
    Four noise methods are available through \code{noise_method}:
    \code{"discrete"}, \code{"gaussian"}, \code{"truncnormal"}, and
    \code{"random"}. The method-specific distributions, automatic scaling
    rules for \code{c}, and type reconstruction details are implemented in
    \code{\link[=inject_noise]{inject_noise()}} and applied column-wise by
    \code{\link[=add_noise]{add_noise()}}.

    Feeder construction is controlled by
    \code{engine_control$feeder_include_observed}. When this value is
    \code{NULL}, the engine follows the original behavior:
    with covariates, subgroup feeder data pool observed and noisy rows;
    without covariates, feeder data use the noisy bootstrap rows only. Setting
    \code{feeder_include_observed = TRUE} or \code{FALSE} overrides this
    default.

  \item Estimate a raw subgroup correlation matrix from the subgroup feeder
    data:
    \deqn{
      R_g^{\mathrm{raw}} =
      \mathrm{cor.shrink}(D_g^{\mathrm{feed}}[V]).
    }
    If this estimate fails, the implementation falls back to
    \eqn{R_{\mathrm{global}}}. With measurement blocks, the same
    between-block attenuation and within-block restoration are applied to the
    subgroup matrix. The subgroup matrix is then blended toward
    the global target:
    \deqn{
      R_g^\star =
      \lambda_g R_g^{\mathrm{raw}} +
      (1 - \lambda_g) R_{\mathrm{global}},
      \qquad
      \lambda_g =
      \frac{n_g^{\mathrm{feed}}}
      {n_g^{\mathrm{feed}} + 30}.
    }
    Larger subgroup feeder sizes therefore rely more heavily on the subgroup
    estimate, while smaller subgroup feeder sizes borrow more from the global
    correlation structure.

  \item Stabilize \eqn{R_g^\star} before simulation. When measurement blocks
    are supplied, within-block submatrices are restored again from the
    observed global reference after subgroup/global blending so that
    positive-definite adjustment does not become the primary determinant of
    item-level structure. The implementation
    replaces missing entries with zero, symmetrizes the matrix, sets the
    diagonal to one, floors eigenvalues below a small tolerance, reconstructs
    the matrix, and rescales it back to unit diagonal. This produces a
    positive-definite correlation matrix suitable for Gaussian simulation.

  \item Allocate final synthetic output rows across subgroups using the
    original subgroup proportions:
    \deqn{
      n_g^{\star} =
      \mathrm{round}(\pi_g n_{\mathrm{aug}}),
    }
    with any rounding discrepancy added to the largest allocated subgroup so
    that \eqn{\sum_g n_g^{\star} = n_{\mathrm{aug}}}.

  \item For each subgroup, simulate Gaussian copula uniforms by drawing
    \deqn{
      Z_g^\star \sim
      \mathcal{N}(0,\; R_g^\star)
    }
    and transforming component-wise:
    \deqn{
      U_g = \Phi(Z_g^\star).
    }
    For variable \eqn{v}, synthetic values are reconstructed through the
    empirical quantile map of the subgroup feeder margin:
    \deqn{
      x_{iv}^{\star(g)} =
      \left(F_{vg}^{\mathrm{feed}}\right)^{-1}(u_{iv}),
    }
    where \eqn{F_{vg}^{\mathrm{feed}}} is the empirical distribution of
    variable \eqn{v} in \eqn{D_g^{\mathrm{feed}}}. Continuous variables use
    empirical quantiles; integer-like variables use discrete quantiles;
    factors and ordered factors are mapped back to valid levels. Numeric
    outputs are clamped to the observed global range, and integer-like
    outputs are reconstructed to match the original storage type when
    \code{round_int = TRUE}.
}

Measurement-aware copula synthesis additionally uses a shared hierarchical
block plan. Plain named lists define leaf measurement blocks and final
structural families. In \code{cscore::composite_list()}-style inputs,
lower-order batteries remain leaves and batteries referenced by a
higher-order composite are combined into one final family. Composites
containing no synthesized variables are recorded as ignored, unblocked
variables become singleton families, and each leaf can belong to only one
final structural family.

Copula measurement awareness has three activation modes:
\enumerate{
  \item If both \code{measurement_blocks} and
    \code{between_block_shrink} are \code{NULL}, the legacy copula path is
    retained without final structural calibration.
  \item If \code{measurement_blocks = NULL} and
    \code{between_block_shrink} is explicitly supplied, every synthesized
    variable is treated as an implicit singleton family. All off-diagonal
    relationships are therefore shrunk and calibrated.
  \item If a partial \code{measurement_blocks} list is supplied, listed
    multi-item families retain within-family protection and every unlisted
    synthesized variable is automatically added as a singleton family.
}

Composite-only shrinkage therefore requires only:
\preformatted{
engine_copula(
  data = dat,
  variables = c("material", "dissatisfaction", "merit"),
  between_block_shrink = 0.50
)
}
A mixed item/composite analysis can instead use:
\preformatted{
engine_copula(
  data = dat,
  variables = c("a1", "a2", "a3", "outcome", "age_score"),
  measurement_blocks = list(attitude = c("a1", "a2", "a3")),
  between_block_shrink = 0.50
)
}
Here \code{outcome} and \code{age_score} are inferred singleton families.

The matrix adjustments above provide the first measurement-aware layer. If
\eqn{M_\lambda} has diagonal and within-family entries equal to one and
between-family entries equal to \eqn{\lambda}, subgroup and global copula
matrices are attenuated as
\deqn{
  \widetilde{R} = R \circ M_\lambda,
}
followed by restoration of observed global within-family submatrices.

A second layer controls realized family-score correlations after subgroup
outputs are stacked. Let \eqn{R_{\mathrm{obs}}} be the correlation matrix of
standardized structural-family scores in the input \code{data}. The final
target is
\deqn{
  R_{\mathrm{target}} = I +
  \lambda (R_{\mathrm{obs}} - I),
}
where \eqn{\lambda} is \code{between_block_shrink}. Twenty-five deterministic
Iman-Conover-style candidate calibrations are generated. For each family,
one row permutation is applied to all of its items, and candidate error is
recomputed from the resulting item data. Selection minimizes maximum
off-diagonal target error and then mean absolute error. The uncalibrated
copula output is candidate zero, so calibration cannot worsen either error
criterion.

Complete-family permutation preserves each family's generated multivariate
item distribution. The final calibration therefore leaves item margins and
support, within-family correlations, lower-order relations within a
higher-order family, alpha, composite reliability, and AVE unchanged.
Covariates are carried through but are not calibration targets; observed,
raw, and calibrated family-to-covariate associations are stored as
diagnostics.

The final synthetic dataset is formed by stacking subgroup outputs. If
\code{covariates} are supplied, each synthetic subgroup block receives the
corresponding covariate values for that subgroup. The returned
\code{models} element stores, for each subgroup, the \pkg{copula}
normal-copula object, the stabilized correlation matrix, and the margin type
\code{"empirical"}. In measurement-aware mode, \code{models$all} also stores
the hierarchy, structural targets, realized raw and calibrated correlations,
target errors, within-family invariance diagnostics, and covariate
diagnostics.

This engine is appropriate when preserving broad joint dependence structure
is more important than fitting a separate conditional model for each target.
It should not be interpreted as a privacy mechanism or as confirmatory
sample-size expansion.
